<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Netilas Yadayim L'Seudah — Betzi'as HaPas<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">(Part 6 — Final: §§ 69–87)</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam Yenahaigem"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM II, 7</span>)</span> — conclusion.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 69 — <i>Birchas HaMazon</i>: Drawing <i>Ratzon</i> Through Speech</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>. The essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> of eating is to merit the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> that will shine in the future — <span class="v">"You give them their food in its time"</span> — <span class="v">"at the time, it shall be said to Yaakov and Yisrael: <span class="h">mah</span> has G-d wrought!"</span> — <span class="v">"how great is Your goodness that You have stored for those who fear You."</span> Therefore the Torah commanded <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> after eating — so each Jew draws upon himself the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> through the holy speech of blessing, thanking, and praising Hashem for his food. All blessings and thanksgivings to His great Name are the aspect of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, for <span class="h">Shemo</span> [His Name] = <span class="h">Ratzon</span> in <span class="h">gematria</span>. All are included in the ten types of melody — blessing, thanksgiving, etc. — all aspects of wondrous <span class="h">devekus</span> and intense yearning. Therefore the essential <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> is over the cup of wine — <span class="mx">"song is only said over wine"</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">devekus</span> and <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 70 — The Three <i>Berachos</i> of <i>Birchas HaMazon</i></div>

<div class="p">The three <span class="h">berachos</span> correspond to the three aspects of inclusiveness. The <span class="kc">first <span class="h">berachah</span></span> — instituted by Moshe when the <span class="h">man</span> descended — the aspect of <span class="h">mah</span>, the upper dwellers. Moshe's comprehension is <span class="h">mah</span> — the lower included in the upper. The <span class="h">man</span>: <span class="v">"They called its name <span class="h">man</span>, for they did not know <span class="h">mah</span> it was."</span></div>

<div class="p">The <span class="kc">second <span class="h">berachah</span></span> — <span class="h">Birchas Ha'aretz</span> — instituted by Yehoshua: the illumination of the lower dwellers, <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span> His glory is revealed primarily in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. Yehoshua is the student — his comprehension is <span class="h">Melo</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="kc">third <span class="h">berachah</span></span> — <span class="h">Boneh Yerushalayim</span> — instituted by Dovid and Shlomo when the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was built: inclusiveness of both = the essential union of the worlds. Through all three, the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating is drawn — <span class="v">"<span class="h">mah</span>"</span> specifically.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 71 — <i>HaTov V'HaMeitiv</i>: Burial of the Slain of Beitar</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">berachah</span> of <span class="h">HaTov V'HaMeitiv</span> — instituted at Yavneh for the slain of Beitar who were given burial. The essential true and eternal good is the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> that each will merit in the future — <span class="v">"how great is Your goodness stored for those who fear You."</span> In the future, all will bless <span class="h">HaTov V'HaMeitiv</span> on everything — understanding that all that passes over a person serves his eternal good.</div>

<div class="p">After the destruction, the Sages added this fourth <span class="h">berachah</span> — drawing <span class="h">Ratzon</span> from the World to Come into this world. When the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> stood, <span class="h">Ratzon</span> flowed through the three <span class="h">berachos</span>. Now: <span class="mx">"a person's table atones"</span> — a fourth <span class="h">berachah</span> is needed.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="kc">Burial is the <span class="h">tikkun</span> of the dead</span> — the sprouting of eternal life: <span class="v">"the dust returns to the earth, and the spirit returns to G-d"</span>. The Midrash: <span class="v">"the day of death is better than the day of birth"</span> — when alive, counted toward death; when dead, counted toward life. But only after burial, for the soul has no rest until then. The higher the soul ascends, the more it illuminates below. The body decomposes in the earth, eliminating the contamination from Adam's sin — whose blemish was through eating from the Tree of Knowledge, blemishing the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="p">When the Sages saw the slain of Beitar were not being permitted burial, they feared hope was lost. The slain of Beitar had brought the destruction closer by lighting candles on the anniversary of the <span class="h">Churban</span>. But when Hashem had mercy through the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s prayers and they were given burial — the Sages saw: <span class="v">"the kindnesses of Hashem have not ended."</span> They instituted <span class="h">HaTov V'HaMeitiv</span> specifically in <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> — because the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> is drawn during eating. <span class="kc">Now after the destruction, we must strengthen with ever stronger <span class="h">Ratzon</span></span> — sustaining ourselves through yearning for the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, reciting the sacrifices: <span class="v">"our lips shall substitute for bulls."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 72 — Yirmiyahu's Lament and Self-Comfort</div>

<div class="p">Yirmiyahu lamented: <span class="v">"My endurance has perished, and my hope from Hashem"</span> <span class="sr">(Lamentations 3:18)</span>. But he comforts himself: <span class="v">"This I take to heart, therefore I have hope: the kindnesses of Hashem have not ended, for His mercies are not consumed. <span class="kc">They are new every morning</span>; great is Your faithfulness"</span> <span class="sr">(Lamentations 3:21-23)</span>. <span class="v">"New every morning"</span> = <span class="h">techiyas hameisim</span> — all souls renewed, living eternal life = the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. Despite everything: <span class="v">"Hashem is my portion, says my soul; therefore I will hope for Him."</span> Now in the depth of exile, each must strengthen in <span class="h">Ratzon</span> — waiting with great yearning. The more <span class="h">Ratzon</span> one draws upon himself amid exile, the more he merits the great good stored for the future.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 73 — <i>Bein HaMetzarim</i>: Mourning, <i>Machlokes</i>, and <i>Mah</i> vs. <i>Lanu</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Bein HaMetzarim</span> — mourning the destruction caused by the <span class="h">meraglim</span>: <span class="mx">"You wept for nothing; I will establish a weeping for generations."</span> <span class="kc">The greatest tragedy is the departure of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>.</span> All sins — the <span class="h">meraglim</span>, the Calf, the ten trials — came through <span class="h">machlokes</span> against Moshe. Now too, the prolonging of exile is through <span class="h">machlokes</span> against the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Yirmiyahu cries: <span class="v">"Remember, Hashem, <span class="h">mah</span> has been done to us"</span>. The Arizal: from <span class="h">mah</span> it became <span class="h">lanu</span> — ego overpowering <span class="h">mah</span>. One must be in the aspect of <span class="h">mah</span>, included in the true <span class="h">Rebbe</span> — Moshe, who said <span class="v">"<span class="h">v'nachnu mah</span>"</span> — nullifying all selfhood. Whoever desires honor creates separation; from <span class="h">mah</span> it becomes <span class="h">lanu</span> = harsh judgments. <span class="v">"Look and see our disgrace"</span> — disgrace is the opposite of honor; whoever chases honor arrives at disgrace. <span class="v">"Our inheritance turned to strangers"</span> — sustenance cannot flow without inclusiveness. Therefore: <span class="v">"Not to us, Hashem, not to us — but to Your Name give glory"</span> — nullifying our <span class="h">lanu</span>, returning all glory to His Name = <span class="v">"Hashem — He is the G-d"</span> = <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 74 — <i>Zimun</i>: "We Wish to Bless!"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Birchas HaZimun</span> was instituted only for <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>: the leader says he wishes to bless — <span class="v">"<span class="h">nevoraich she'achalnu mishelo</span>"</span> — and our Sages introduced saying beforehand: <span class="kc"><span class="h">"Rabosai, mir villen bentshen!"</span></span> Open your eyes and see the wonders of our Sages: specifically for <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> they instituted speaking the wondrous <span class="h">Ratzon</span> aloud — because the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> shines specifically during eating.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 75 — Why Three for <i>Zimun</i></div>

<div class="p">Three correspond to the three aspects: upper, lower, and their union — the three <span class="h">Kedushos</span>: <span class="mx">"holy in the heavens"</span> (upper), <span class="mx">"holy on the earth"</span> (lower), <span class="mx">"holy forever"</span> (inclusiveness). Only then: <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span> The greatest is the <span class="h">Rav</span>; the two illuminate one another. As the <span class="h">Mishnah</span> in <span class="h">Avos</span> praises three who eat at one table and speak words of Torah.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 76 — <i>Zimun</i> with Ten: The Name in the <i>Ratzon</i></div>

<div class="p">When ten eat together, they mention the Name: <span class="v">"<span class="h">Nevoraich Elokeinu</span>."</span> <span class="h">Zimun</span> is the expression of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> itself — not the <span class="h">berachah</span>, but the declaration of desire to bless. <span class="h">Ratzon</span> is above all Names — at its root, in <span class="h">Atik</span>, in the innermost <span class="h">Keser</span>. To draw the Name down into the <span class="h">Ratzon</span> — drawing from concealment to revelation — requires ten, corresponding to the ten <span class="h">Sefiros</span> from <span class="h">Keser</span> (<span class="h">Ayeh</span>) to <span class="h">Malchus</span> (<span class="h">Melo</span>). <span class="h">Rabbainu</span> stated that this Torah teaching contains all ten <span class="h">Sefiros</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Similarly, <span class="h">Kedushah</span>, <span class="h">Keser</span>, and <span class="h">Borchu</span> require ten — to proclaim publicly, arousing upper and lower together. Individual blessings accomplish this privately; the great public proclamation requires ten. Moshe himself: <span class="v">"You are standing today, all of you, before Hashem your G-d — your heads, your tribes… to your wood-chopper and your water-drawer"</span> — ten levels from highest to lowest.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 77 — <i>Makifin</i>, Free Choice, and the <i>Tzadik</i>'s Ascent</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Ratzon</span> derives from <span class="h">makifin</span> that cannot be grasped = <span class="h">mah</span>. One must strive to bring the <span class="h">makifin</span> inside — through strengthening the <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, yearning constantly for Hashem. Then one merits to understand what was previously incomprehensible = grasping the <span class="h">makifin</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">tzadik</span> differs from the angels: the angel is <span class="h">omeid</span> (standing still); the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, through free choice, ascends constantly. Each higher ascent illuminates below further. The angels ask <span class="v">"<span class="h">Ayeh</span>?"</span> — but the <span class="h">tzadik</span> knows what is hidden from them, and even more — yet knows that <span class="kc">"the end of knowledge is that we do not know."</span> He informs the angels that they do not know what he knows, and even what he knows remains below the ultimate unknowing. In the future: <span class="mx">"the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> will be within the angels' circle, and the angels will ask them: <span class="h">mah pa'al El</span>?"</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Rabbainu</span> said: Where the philosophers' knowledge ends entirely — beyond the celestial spheres — there the knowledge of true <span class="h">Kabbalah</span> begins. But even the great <span class="h">Mekubalim</span> who revealed wondrous comprehensions in the world of <span class="h">Atzilus</span> and above — even there, one can ascend further and further. Everyone agrees the ultimate knowledge is "that we do not know" — <span class="kc">but not all faces are equal</span>: most do not even know what they do not know.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 78 — Moshe's Departure and Yehoshua</div>

<div class="p">Moshe yearned for <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and prayed immensely. The obstacle was <span class="h">machlokes</span> — <span class="h">mei merivah</span>. <span class="v">"<span class="h">Rav lach</span> — enough for you; do not speak to Me further about this"</span> = silence = <span class="h">makifin</span> too exalted for this lifetime. <span class="v">"Be silent — thus it arose in thought"</span> — as answered regarding Rabbi Akiva's death.</div>

<div class="p">Immediately: <span class="v">"Command Yehoshua and strengthen him."</span> <span class="kc">Specifically through Moshe's departure</span> — ascending to the highest <span class="h">makifin</span> impossible to reach while in a body — Yehoshua received the power to enter <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. Through this, for all generations: even when the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> is destroyed and the <span class="h">sitra achra</span> expands enormously — awesome <span class="h">tzadikim</span> will arise even at the very end. Even when the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> are forced to depart — through this very thing, salvation comes. <span class="mx">"Greater are the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in death than in life."</span> Through their ascent, they illuminate their students from generation to generation.</div>

<div class="p">The Zohar says: through one <span class="h">bais haknesses</span> and one town returning in truth, <span class="h">Mashiach</span> will come. Despite all the <span class="h">machlokes</span> — Hashem will complete His purpose. The true <span class="h">tzadik</span> illuminates the <span class="h">da'as</span> in wondrous ways until all the lower dwellers are awakened, returning to Hashem, gathering into one synagogue, saying the <span class="h">devarim shebikdushah</span> with intention and connection to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — and then: <span class="v">"a redeemer shall come to Tzion"</span> — through <span class="v">"My spirit that is upon you and My words that I have placed in your mouth shall not depart from your mouth or the mouth of your children or your children's children… from now and forever."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 79 — "<i>Nachamu, Nachamu</i>" and the Three <i>Aichahs</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Comfort, comfort My people"</span> — Hashem charges Israel to comfort one another: <span class="kc">each speaking with his fellow about <span class="h">yiras Shamayim</span></span> daily. <span class="v">"Her host is filled"</span> = filled with people of <span class="h">da'as</span>. <span class="v">"Her sin is pardoned [<span class="h">nirtzah</span>]"</span> = transformed into <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. <span class="v">"She has taken double"</span> = the departure of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (= double the destruction). <span class="v">"A voice calls in the wilderness"</span> — no despair even for one lost in the wilderness of sins. <span class="v">"Every valley raised, every mountain lowered"</span> = upper below, lower above. <span class="v">"The glory of Hashem shall be revealed, and all flesh shall see."</span></div>

<div class="p">The three <span class="h">Aichahs</span>: the first (<span class="v">"how she sits alone"</span>) mourns the exile from Yerushalayim; the second (<span class="v">"how He has clouded"</span>) mourns the destruction itself; the third (<span class="v">"how the gold is dimmed"</span>) mourns the departure of the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Yoshiyahu). Yirmiyahu said <span class="v">"I am the man"</span> — taking all upon himself, teaching every person to say likewise about his own sins. Yet he strengthens: <span class="v">"the kindnesses of Hashem have not ended… they are new every morning."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 80 — 15 Av and Yom Kippur</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"There were no days as good for Israel as the 15th of Av and Yom Kippur."</span> On the 15th of Av, the deaths of the desert generation ceased = beginning of the <span class="h">tikkun</span> of the <span class="h">meraglim</span>. Yom Kippur = requesting <span class="h">Selach Na</span> for the same sin. On the 15th of Av, the slain of Beitar were given burial and <span class="h">HaTov V'HaMeitiv</span> was instituted. All interconnects: the 15th of Av = <span class="h">tikkun</span> of the <span class="h">meraglim</span> = the beginning of the building of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> = the drawing of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 81 — <i>Parshas Eikev</i>: At the "Heel" of Existence</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"<span class="h">V'hayah eikev</span> tishme'un"</span> <span class="sr">(Deuteronomy 7:12)</span>. Throughout <span class="h">Devarim</span>, Moshe illuminates the holy <span class="h">da'as</span>: Hashem's love for Israel, His desire that they know Him from this physical world. He subdued Sichon and Og (rooted in the fallen angels who said <span class="v">"What is man that You remember him?"</span>) — and strengthened Yehoshua: <span class="v">"your eyes have seen all that Hashem your G-d has done to these two kings — so shall He do to all the kingdoms."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Do not fear them, for Hashem your G-d fights for you."</span> As <span class="h">Rabbainu</span> said: <span class="mx">"A person must cross a very narrow bridge in this world, and the main thing is not to be afraid at all."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"<span class="h">Eikev</span>"</span> = the heel/bottom: when a person or generation is at the lowest point — <span class="h">ikvesa d'Meshicha</span> — if you heed the <span class="h">da'as</span> (= <span class="h">mishpatim</span>), knowing Hashem is still with you: <span class="v">"He will keep the covenant… bless you… bless the fruit of your womb and your land"</span> — sustenance through the inclusiveness of the worlds, in the aspect of blessing = <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 82 — Plural to Singular: Sustenance for Each</div>

<div class="p">Moshe speaks first in the plural (<span class="v">"if you heed"</span>) — drawing <span class="h">da'as</span> requires the many. Then switches to the singular for the promise (<span class="v">"He will bless you"</span>) — sustenance is distributed to each individual. The <span class="h">man</span> was ground in the <span class="h">shchakim</span> and distributed to each as appropriate. The essential blessing: each receives with <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, happy with his portion, believing this is Hashem's good will for him.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 83 — The Midrash: Reward <i>B'Eikev</i></div>

<div class="p">The Midrash: Israel asked, "When will You give us reward?" Hashem: <span class="mx">"You eat the fruits of your <span class="h">mitzvos</span> now; the reward — I give you <span class="h">b'eikev</span> [in the end]."</span> The essential great good is stored for the World to Come. <span class="v">"<span class="h">V'shamru</span> — Hashem will keep for you"</span> = He is keeping the reward.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 84 — <i>Devarim Shebikdushah</i> from the <i>Meraglim</i></div>

<div class="p">The law that <span class="h">devarim shebikdushah</span> require ten is derived from the <span class="h">meraglim</span>: <span class="v">"until when for this evil <span class="h">eidah</span>?"</span> — ten, excluding Yehoshua and Calev. Through a <span class="h">gezeirah shavah</span> to <span class="v">"I shall be sanctified among the children of Israel"</span> — <span class="kc">holy matters require ten.</span></div>

<div class="p">What wondrous kindness: from the evil assembly that caused all destruction, Hashem taught Israel to form holy assemblies of ten — proclaiming the holy <span class="h">da'as</span>: <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span> Through this, the evil assembly is uprooted from its root. The counsel of Hashem stands forever.</div>

